inline_comments.body:
`buildTripForVehicleReferences` only ever appends the vehicle's own agency to `references.Agencies`, even though `routeReferenceForTrip`/`routeReferenceByID` (added in this PR) can now resolve the active trip's route from a *different* agency than the vehicle's own.

`TestTripForVehicleHandler_RouteFromAnotherAgency` (added in this PR) already exercises this exact scenario — a route from `OTHERAGENCY` — and confirms the route reference correctly carries the `OTHERAGENCY_` prefix. But the test never asserts on `references.Agencies`, so it doesn't catch that `OTHERAGENCY` itself is missing from that list. A client resolving `references.routes[0].agencyId` against `references.agencies` won't find a matching entry.

`arrival_and_departure_for_stop_handler.go:434-443` already has the right pattern for this — fetch and append the route's own agency when it differs from the primary one:
```go
if route.AgencyID != stopAgency.ID {
    routeAgency, err := api.GtfsManager.GtfsDB.Queries.GetAgency(ctx, route.AgencyID)
    if err == nil {
        references.Agencies = append(references.Agencies, models.AgencyReferenceFromDatabase(&routeAgency))
    } else {
        api.Logger.Warn("failed to fetch route agency for reference", "agencyID", route.AgencyID, "error", err)
    }
}
```
Worth applying the same here, and extending `TestTripForVehicleHandler_RouteFromAnotherAgency` to assert `references.Agencies` contains the route's agency.
